The part a licence file alone does not tell you
-----------------------------------------------

**The licence on our own code is not the whole licence picture for a
distributed binary.** [D6](docs/decisions/D6-license.md) ruled this per-crate,
and the split is deliberate:

| Component | Our own code | What governs a *distributed binary* |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `antseal-core` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | permissive — and **must stay so** |
| `antseal-anchor` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | permissive |
| `verifier-web` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | permissive |
| `antseal-net` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | **effectively AGPL-3.0** (see below) |
| `antseal-cli` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | **effectively AGPL-3.0** (see below) |

A CLI built with the `ant-backend` feature — the only build that can actually
seal — links `ant-core` and `ant-protocol` through `antseal-net`, and that
pulls in **three copyleft crates**, measured in the resolved graph on
2026-08-17 rather than taken from any list:

| crate | licence | arrives via |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `self_encryption 0.36.0` | GPL-3.0, no linking exception | `ant-core` |
| `evmlib 0.9.0` | GPL-3.0 | `ant-protocol` |
| `saorsa-core 0.26.2` | **AGPL-3.0** | `ant-protocol` |

Such a binary is therefore a combined work containing AGPL-3.0 code, and **its
distribution must satisfy AGPL-3.0 regardless of the permissive licence on our
own source**. AGPL-3.0 is strictly stronger than GPL-3.0: its §13 adds a
remote-network source-offer term. For antseal's actual shape — a binary you run
locally, acting as a client of the Autonomi network — the practical obligation
is the same as GPL-3.0, because §13 binds whoever *offers* a modified program
to remote users, not someone running their own copy. It is still a different
licence with a different term, which is why it is named here rather than
rounded down to "GPL".

**All three are absent from a default build.** Verified both directions with
`cargo tree -i --edges normal`: with `--features ant-backend` each has a path
to `antseal-cli`; without it, none does, and the default graph resolves 213
packages with no GPL or AGPL among them. That is what keeps `antseal-core`,
`antseal-anchor` and `verifier-web` — and therefore the WASM verifier page —
genuinely permissive.

One caveat the per-crate table cannot show: `antseal-anchor` depends on
`antseal-net` unconditionally. Its own code is permissive and it is clean in a
default build, but a distributed binary with `ant-backend` enabled puts
`antseal-anchor` inside the combined work too.

Dual-licensing our own code is what keeps that contained: every line we write
stays reusable under MIT or Apache-2.0, and the whole arrangement collapses to
plain permissive the moment the GPL-3.0 dependency goes away.

Two consequences worth stating plainly, because they are easy to get wrong:

1. **The crates.io metadata is accurate but incomplete.** The published
   `antseal-net` and `antseal-cli` records declare `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, which
   correctly describes *our* code. It does not describe the distribution
   obligation above. Read this file, not only the registry field.

   Removing `self_encryption` would **not** dissolve the obligation, and D6
   originally implied it would. `evmlib` and `saorsa-core` arrive by a
   different path — `ant-protocol`, not `ant-core` — so all three would have
   to go. D6's 2026-08-17 addendum corrects that sentence at source.

2. **`self_encryption` must never become a direct `antseal-core` dependency.**
   That would pull GPL-3.0 into the permissive core and therefore into the
   WASM verifier page, destroying the split and the "hostable on any static
   host" premise that `MVP-SPEC.md` line 157 rests on. D6 records the options
   if the need arises; vendoring is not one of them, because vendoring keeps
   the GPL.

Where the licence files live, and why `verifier-web/` has none
--------------------------------------------------------------

`LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE` sit at the repository root and are copied
into each **publishable** crate (`antseal-core`, `antseal-anchor`,
`antseal-net`, `antseal-cli`) so a published `.crate` carries its own licence
text.

**`verifier-web/` deliberately holds no licence file**, and the absence is
enforced rather than accidental. `crates/antseal-wasm/tests/page_template.rs`
asserts that directory holds *exactly* `index.template.html`, because a second
file there "would silently widen what the R18 wording scan covers" — the page
template is a frozen-wording surface, not an ordinary source directory. Q29
originally added both licence files there per its own Accept row; the gate
caught the conflict and the guard wins, since it is landed, tested, and
protects a freeze.

The redistribution grant `MVP-SPEC.md` line 157 requires for the page is
unaffected: the root files licence the whole repository, `verifier-web`'s own
code is `MIT OR Apache-2.0` like everything else, and nothing about hosting the
built page depends on a licence file sitting beside the template. **Carrying
licence text in the DEPLOYED page artifact is a separate question and is owed
by Q31**, which owns what the release actually publishes — it is not satisfied,
and must not be assumed satisfied, by this file.

Third-party code retains its own licences. `docs/dependency-policy.md` and
`deny.toml` govern what is admissible in the dependency graph.
